{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## /rebar-debug final report — fix pushed as Gerrit change 1956\n\n### Problem\nThe close gate's undeclared-paths refusal advertised a remedy (\"declare the paths and retry\")\nwhose second half cannot succeed, because declaring them stales the attestation.\n\n### Proven root cause\nThe materiality test hashes the whole `file_impact` list\n(`llm/plan_review/material_diff.py:110` `material_basis`, `:155` `_hash_component`), so any\nmutation changes the digest. Runtime-executed, not read:\n\n```\nsigned  hash: c3ec16dd33b8418d\nwidened hash: c251cc50b5cd4233   CHANGED? True   <- purely additive\nremoved hash: 9708be1d47b72913   CHANGED? True   <- removal\n```\n\nAdditive and destructive edits are equally \"different\", so nothing downstream could distinguish\nthem even in principle.\n\n### Scope correction — the gates are NOT the defect\nThe original framing (and my own first analysis, retracted above) treated this as two gates\ncontradicting each other and proposed exempting additive widenings. That was wrong:\n`llm/plan_review/read_set.py:4` builds the attestation's scope \"from the ticket's declared\n``file_impact`` ∪ the files the review CITED\", so widening it retroactively broadens what an\nexisting signature claims to cover; and `file_impact` feeds review at four sites\n(`pass1.py:242`, `det_lint.py:267`, `det_invariants.py:35`, `det_gate_rules.py`), so a new path\nCAN introduce new findings. Both refusals are correct. **The only defect is the message.**\n\n### Defect origin — `cec6-0a0e-38a9-4234`, class `plan_defect`\n`git blame -w -C -M -L 302,310` puts every line of the refusal on `746c1ba2e96`\n(\"Add attach-commits verb and impact-vs-diff gate\", 2026-08-12, `rebar-ticket: cec6-0a0e-38a9-4234`).\n\nChecked for a carrier: `git log -S'which the recorded file_impact does not declare' --all`\nreturns **exactly one** commit, and its diffstat is +126 on this file — it CREATED the check and\nauthored the message. Not a rename, reformat or move.\n\nApplied the date test to rule out an unmet obligation. `stale-material` was introduced by\n`2873a91648` on **2026-06-23**, roughly **seven weeks BEFORE** the message was written on\n**2026-08-12**. So no later change created this obligation — the gate the remedy collides with\nalready existed and was already documented when the remedy was authored. That makes this an\n**omission at origin**, not a regression and not a missed consumer update.\n\nSanity check per protocol: could a reviewer of `cec6` plausibly have caught it? Yes — the\ninteraction was fully present in the tree at review time. That is what makes `plan_defect` the\nright class rather than `preexisting`.\n\nThe close will carry `--caused-by=cec6-0a0e-38a9-4234 --class=plan_defect` explicitly, so blame\nauto-derivation does not add a competing guessed edge beside the proven one.\n\n### Test\n`tests/unit/test_close_precheck_message_fb8b.py`, two cases. Observed **RED first** for the right\nreason (the fixture asserts it reached the undeclared-paths branch before asserting on wording),\nthen GREEN. **Mutation check**: re-seeded the defect by deleting the re-attestation clause, the\ntest returned to RED, restored to GREEN — so it is not a tautology. The second case pins the\nexisting diagnosis (sha, paths, `set-file-impact`, `--force`) so a future rewrite cannot silently\ndrop it.\n\n### Fix\n`_commands/close_precheck.py` — the refusal now states that the work went beyond the reviewed\nplan, that widening `file_impact` is a MATERIAL plan change which invalidates the attestation\n(`stale-material`), and that `rebar review-plan` must be re-run before the close can succeed.\n**No behavioural change to either gate.**\n\n### Scope & safety\nCaller sweep: the message is a leaf string in a `raise CommandError` with no callers depending on\nits wording — no test pinned the old text (`grep 'and retry' tests/` found only my own new file's\ndocstring quoting it). Restores no behaviour and creates none; adds no dependency; weakens no\ntest. 481 tests pass in the close/gate/file_impact/precheck subset. `make lint`, `make typecheck`\ngreen; complexity ratchet `new=0 increased=0 stale=0`; file at 740/800 LOC.\n\n### Siblings\nPrecedent, not sibling: `db1c` closed this same class for the bounded-recovery message, and its\ndocstring names the identical shape — \"the advertised remedy for one close gate is a guaranteed\nfailure of the other, and the message says nothing about that trade.\" Since two gate messages\nhave now been found advertising remedies that collide with a sibling gate, a targeted sweep of\nthe remaining close/claim gate refusals for the same defect is worth doing. **Not swept in this\nchange** — recorded here as residual rather than silently skipped.\n\n### Replication\nThe original five-step reproduction now surfaces the re-attestation requirement at step 3, so the\nauthor learns at the first refusal what the second will demand. The loop still exists — correctly\n— but is no longer misleading.\n\n### Status\nGerrit **1956**: `LLM-Review +1`, awaiting `Verified`. Attestation re-earned after the scope\nrewrite (`rebar review-plan --status` -> `certified`, currency basis `file_impact`). Not closing\nuntil CI is green and the change has merged."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787253245713010001,"uuid":"fc264b1b-a9c7-421d-9772-9269c05eb9fd"}